WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.582
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.882 --> 00:00:08.572
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.872 --> 00:00:16.064
Unit fifty-three: Justice and Punishment. In this unit, you will debate the purposes of punishment.

4
00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:18.597
restorative justice.

5
00:00:19.297 --> 00:00:24.686
restorative justice means an approach that repairs harm by bringing offenders and victims together.

6
00:00:24.986 --> 00:00:28.298
Restorative justice can help victims feel heard.

7
00:00:28.998 --> 00:00:32.509
Your turn. Say: restorative justice.

8
00:00:35.209 --> 00:00:36.698
restorative justice.

9
00:00:37.683 --> 00:00:39.091
rehabilitation.

10
00:00:39.791 --> 00:00:44.391
rehabilitation means helping offenders change and return to society.

11
00:00:44.691 --> 00:00:47.584
Education in prison supports rehabilitation.

12
00:00:48.284 --> 00:00:51.691
Your turn. Say: rehabilitation.

13
00:00:54.391 --> 00:00:55.671
rehabilitation.

14
00:00:56.683 --> 00:00:57.650
deterrence.

15
00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:02.172
deterrence means discouraging crime through fear of punishment.

16
00:01:02.472 --> 00:01:05.853
Some argue that harsh sentences provide deterrence.

17
00:01:06.553 --> 00:01:09.240
Your turn. Say: deterrence.

18
00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:13.023
deterrence.

19
00:01:14.017 --> 00:01:15.436
incarceration.

20
00:01:16.136 --> 00:01:19.145
incarceration means keeping someone in prison.

21
00:01:19.445 --> 00:01:23.628
Long incarceration can make reintegration harder.

22
00:01:24.328 --> 00:01:27.735
Your turn. Say: incarceration.

23
00:01:30.435 --> 00:01:31.830
incarceration.

24
00:01:32.817 --> 00:01:33.992
recidivism.

25
00:01:34.692 --> 00:01:38.340
recidivism means returning to crime after punishment.

26
00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:40.907
Job training reduces recidivism.

27
00:01:41.607 --> 00:01:44.700
Your turn. Say: recidivism.

28
00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:48.413
recidivism.

29
00:01:49.417 --> 00:01:50.580
death penalty.

30
00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:54.627
death penalty means execution as a legal punishment.

31
00:01:54.927 --> 00:01:57.716
Many countries have abolished the death penalty.

32
00:01:58.416 --> 00:02:01.428
Your turn. Say: death penalty.

33
00:02:04.128 --> 00:02:05.222
death penalty.

34
00:02:06.217 --> 00:02:07.125
parole.

35
00:02:07.825 --> 00:02:11.531
parole means early release from prison under supervision.

36
00:02:11.831 --> 00:02:14.632
He was released on parole after five years.

37
00:02:15.332 --> 00:02:18.205
Your turn. Say: parole.

38
00:02:20.905 --> 00:02:21.755
parole.

39
00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:24.250
sentencing guideline.

40
00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:30.235
sentencing guideline means official rules that help judges decide punishments.

41
00:02:30.535 --> 00:02:33.963
Sentencing guidelines aim to ensure consistency.

42
00:02:34.663 --> 00:02:37.989
Your turn. Say: sentencing guideline.

43
00:02:40.689 --> 00:02:42.201
sentencing guideline.

44
00:02:43.217 --> 00:02:45.286
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:45.586 --> 00:02:48.259
Rithy, what do you think prisons are for?

46
00:02:49.550 --> 00:02:53.735
Punishment, mainly. But rehabilitation, too, should be a priority.

47
00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:56.608
Why rehabilitation?

48
00:02:57.917 --> 00:03:04.458
Because most prisoners eventually return to society. Without support, recidivism stays high.

49
00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:08.272
Some would argue that deterrence matters most.

50
00:03:09.583 --> 00:03:15.034
Possibly. Far more effective, though, is the certainty of being caught, according to research.

51
00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:18.048
And restorative justice?

52
00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:25.578
For minor crimes, it seems promising. Victims, especially, often value being heard.

53
00:03:26.883 --> 00:03:29.347
Grammar focus: Fronting (mastery).

54
00:03:29.647 --> 00:03:32.877
Fronting gives your key ideas extra emphasis.

55
00:03:33.177 --> 00:03:38.965
Rehabilitation, too, should be a priority. Far more effective is the certainty of being caught.

56
00:03:39.265 --> 00:03:42.228
Victims, especially, value being heard.

57
00:03:42.528 --> 00:03:47.743
Fronting with inversion ('Far more effective is ...') creates emphasis and contrast in debates.

58
00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:51.913
Now read and listen. What Is Punishment For?.

59
00:03:52.213 --> 00:03:55.420
Societies have long debated the purpose of punishment.

60
00:03:56.717 --> 00:04:01.909
Some emphasise retribution and deterrence; others prioritise rehabilitation.

61
00:04:03.217 --> 00:04:08.839
Research suggests that the certainty of being caught deters crime more than the severity of sentences.

62
00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:15.029
Long periods of incarceration can weaken family ties and job prospects.

63
00:04:16.317 --> 00:04:21.880
Programmes offering education and work training have been shown to reduce recidivism.

64
00:04:23.183 --> 00:04:30.384
Restorative justice brings victims and offenders together to repair harm, particularly for minor offences.

65
00:04:31.683 --> 00:04:38.675
The death penalty remains controversial, and many countries have abolished it, while others retain it.

66
00:04:39.983 --> 00:04:46.255
Most systems now use sentencing guidelines and parole to balance fairness and public safety.

67
00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:50.260
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:53.222
Returning to crime after punishment is blank.

69
00:04:53.522 --> 00:04:58.081
A, recidivism. B, parole. C, deterrence.

70
00:05:02.083 --> 00:05:04.838
The answer is A: recidivism.

71
00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:08.268
Which sentence uses fronting?

72
00:05:08.568 --> 00:05:15.577
A, Education is also important.. B, Education, too, is important.. C, Is education important?.

73
00:05:19.583 --> 00:05:23.905
The answer is B: Education, too, is important..

74
00:05:25.417 --> 00:05:28.473
Far more important blank the certainty of punishment.

75
00:05:28.773 --> 00:05:31.602
A, are. B, is. C, be.

76
00:05:35.617 --> 00:05:37.756
The answer is B: is.

77
00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:42.503
Early release from prison under supervision is blank.

78
00:05:42.803 --> 00:05:48.941
A, parole. B, incarceration. C, restorative justice.

79
00:05:52.950 --> 00:05:54.950
The answer is A: parole.

80
00:05:56.450 --> 00:05:59.587
According to the text, what deters crime more?

81
00:05:59.887 --> 00:06:06.652
A, severe sentences. B, the certainty of being caught. C, long incarceration.

82
00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:14.414
The answer is B: the certainty of being caught.

83
00:06:15.917 --> 00:06:22.841
Now it's your turn. Part one: Should the main goal of prisons be punishment or rehabilitation?

84
00:06:28.141 --> 00:06:34.170
Part two: Use fronting: give your position, two reasons and one counter-argument.

85
00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:54.793
Rehabilitation, above all, should be the goal. Most prisoners return to society, and education reduces reoffending. Some argue that victims deserve retribution; far more meaningful, I believe, is preventing future victims.

86
00:06:55.093 --> 00:06:57.034
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:06:58.050 --> 00:07:02.734
Great work today! You learned eight new words and fronting (mastery).

88
00:07:03.034 --> 00:07:07.974
Today's value is responsibility. Help at home and do your jobs.

89
00:07:08.274 --> 00:07:11.205
See you in Unit fifty-four. Goodbye!

90
00:07:11.505 --> 00:07:14.076
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

